# Neural Machine Translation (English to French)

A small sequence to sequence model that translates simple English sentences into French. Built with PyTorch using a GRU encoder decoder with Bahdanau attention, the same general architecture used in NMT papers before transformers became the standard.

This is a self contained toy project. Instead of downloading a large parallel corpus, the dataset is generated from sentence templates directly in `data.py`, so the whole thing runs without any internet access or external files.

## Project structure

```
nmt_project/
    data.py        builds the English/French sentence pairs and vocabularies
    model.py        the encoder, attention module, decoder, and seq2seq wrapper
    train.py        trains the model and saves the weights and vocabularies
    translate.py    loads the trained model and translates new sentences
```

Each file has one job, so you can read or change the data generation, the architecture, or the training loop without touching the others.

## How to run it

Install the one dependency:

```
pip install torch
```

Train the model, this creates `nmt_model.pt` and `vocabs.pkl` in the same folder:

```
python3 train.py
```

Then translate a few sample sentences:

```
python3 translate.py
```

You can also run `python3 data.py` on its own if you just want to see what the generated dataset looks like.

## About the dataset

The corpus is built from sentence templates (subject plus adjective, and subject plus verb plus object) covering six subjects, eight adjectives, and twelve verb/object combinations. That works out to 120 sentence pairs total, split 108 for training and 12 for validation. It is small on purpose so training finishes in seconds on a CPU, but that also means the vocabulary is limited to around 45 English words and 52 French words. Feeding it a sentence with words outside that vocabulary will just skip the unknown words rather than fail.

## Results from the actual run

Training for 60 epochs on this dataset:

```
epoch 1  train loss 3.8140  val loss 3.5258
epoch 20 train loss 0.0645  val loss 0.1516
epoch 40 train loss 0.0094  val loss 0.0237
epoch 60 train loss 0.0041  val loss 0.0106
```

Sample translations after training:

```
i am happy      -> je suis heureux
she is tired    -> elle est fatigue
we like coffee  -> nous aime le cafe
they read books -> ils lis des livres
you are busy    -> tu es occupe
```

Most of these are correct, though a couple have subject verb agreement slips ("nous aime" should be "nous aimons"). That is expected from a model trained on only 108 sentences. With a real corpus of a few thousand diverse sentence pairs this would clean up a lot.

## Where this could go next

Swapping the GRU for an LSTM, adding beam search instead of greedy decoding in `translate.py`, or training on a real dataset like Tatoeba or Europarl would all be natural next steps if you wanted to push this past a toy demo.
